1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,818
Imagine a neighbor asks where they
can get help using a computer.

2
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:07,455
You may already have a familiar library,
community center,

3
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:11,930
or veterans' organization in mind.
Use that experience as a starting point,

4
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:17,550
then verify what is available now. Today's
search tools answer in full sentences,

5
00:00:18,270 --> 00:00:23,290
so our first skill is asking well, and our
second is checking what the answer says.

6
00:00:23,890 --> 00:00:26,510
Our task is to find a
suitable public service,

7
00:00:27,150 --> 00:00:30,830
record enough evidence to revisit it,
and organize a fictional handout

8
00:00:30,830 --> 00:00:34,890
without exposing private information.
You can use a different local service

9
00:00:34,890 --> 00:00:38,931
that matters to you. The names and details
in this demonstration are examples,

10
00:00:39,470 --> 00:00:41,853
not a claim about any
particular organization.

11
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:43,870
A useful search

12
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:47,652
ends with a supported decision,
not just an answer that sounds confident.

13
00:00:49,346 --> 00:00:51,466
Search tools now answer in sentences.

14
00:00:52,746 --> 00:00:55,786
Google may show an AI
Overview above the results,

15
00:00:56,406 --> 00:00:59,226
and AI Mode lets you keep
asking follow-up questions.

16
00:01:00,146 --> 00:01:03,358
Both work best when you ask the
way you would ask a librarian.

17
00:01:04,126 --> 00:01:08,006
Say what you need, where you are,
and what matters to you.

18
00:01:08,986 --> 00:01:13,079
Where can I get free computer help near
Beckley, West Virginia, in the evening?

19
00:01:13,446 --> 00:01:15,826
is far more useful than computer help.

20
00:01:15,826 --> 00:01:18,306
If the first answer is too broad,

21
00:01:18,726 --> 00:01:22,146
ask a follow-up, such as which
of these are open on Saturdays.

22
00:01:23,506 --> 00:01:26,511
Pause and write a question for
your chosen community task.

23
00:01:27,226 --> 00:01:31,326
Include the service, the place,
and one detail that matters to you.

24
00:01:32,266 --> 00:01:34,370
Then ask one follow-up.

25
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,920
An AI answer is a quick summary
written from many pages.

26
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:43,980
It can sound sure and still be wrong,
out of date, or about a different town.

27
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:46,760
So treat it as a lead, not a source.

28
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:52,240
Look for the links beside or beneath it,
and open the organization's own page.

29
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:57,545
Ask who published it, when it was updated,
and whether it answers your question.

30
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:00,497
If the summary and the page disagree,

31
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:05,760
trust the responsible organization, and
confirm hours by phone before you travel.

32
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:09,780
Never type private
details into a question,

33
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:14,560
such as a Social Security number,
a bank number, or medical information.

34
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:18,120
Pause, open one source from your answer,

35
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:21,820
and find one detail it
confirms and one it does not.

36
00:02:23,538 --> 00:02:27,113
Suppose you find a useful service
today and need it again next month.

37
00:02:27,618 --> 00:02:30,378
A note saying good website
will not be enough.

38
00:02:31,158 --> 00:02:32,285
Record the organization,

39
00:02:32,638 --> 00:02:33,388
the page title,

40
00:02:33,718 --> 00:02:37,967
the web address, the date you checked it,
and the particular fact you plan to use.

41
00:02:39,078 --> 00:02:40,943
If an AI answer led you to the page,

42
00:02:41,298 --> 00:02:43,733
record the page it pointed to,
not the summary.

43
00:02:44,458 --> 00:02:45,773
A bookmark helps you return,

44
00:02:46,118 --> 00:02:50,159
but it does not freeze the page or prove
that the information remains current.

45
00:02:50,618 --> 00:02:54,400
In your handout, distinguish a checked
fact from your own interpretation.

46
00:02:54,838 --> 00:02:56,859
If the page does not answer a question,

47
00:02:56,918 --> 00:02:58,118
write needs confirmation

48
00:02:58,118 --> 00:03:02,778
rather than filling the gap with a guess.
This is the digital equivalent

49
00:03:02,778 --> 00:03:05,990
of writing the source beside a
number in a household notebook.

50
00:03:07,627 --> 00:03:09,648
An online form is a structured request.

51
00:03:10,267 --> 00:03:12,909
Read its purpose and required
fields before typing.

52
00:03:13,707 --> 00:03:15,022
In our course practice form,

53
00:03:15,187 --> 00:03:19,073
choose the kind of computer help needed
and a fictional contact preference.

54
00:03:19,347 --> 00:03:22,093
Review the entries before
selecting the final button.

55
00:03:22,867 --> 00:03:23,712
On a real service,

56
00:03:23,987 --> 00:03:26,940
ask whether a sensitive detail
is necessary for the task,

57
00:03:27,207 --> 00:03:29,228
and verify the site before entering it.

58
00:03:29,687 --> 00:03:32,027
Our practice does not send a real request.

59
00:03:33,007 --> 00:03:34,907
That distinction matters: seeing

60
00:03:34,907 --> 00:03:38,897
a confirmation message in a simulator
proves that you completed the exercise,

61
00:03:39,147 --> 00:03:41,375
not that an organization
received anything.

62
00:03:42,067 --> 00:03:43,287
Pause, complete

63
00:03:43,287 --> 00:03:46,966
the fictional form, and explain what
you deliberately left out and why.

64
00:03:48,605 --> 00:03:51,765
Think of a folder as a labeled
place to put related work.

65
00:03:52,445 --> 00:03:53,425
Create a practice

66
00:03:53,425 --> 00:03:57,345
folder for the community resource
task and use a descriptive file name,

67
00:03:57,645 --> 00:04:02,025
such as library-help-draft.

68
00:04:02,025 --> 00:04:05,625
A file saved on this computer may
not appear on another computer.

69
00:04:06,485 --> 00:04:09,545
A cloud file belongs to
a service and an account,

70
00:04:10,085 --> 00:04:13,145
and a synced folder may keep
copies in more than one place.

71
00:04:13,905 --> 00:04:18,865
Before moving to another device, check the
account and the saving or syncing status.

72
00:04:20,245 --> 00:04:22,473
Reopen the file from
the intended location.

73
00:04:23,345 --> 00:04:25,521
That simple test catches a common mistake:

74
00:04:25,705 --> 00:04:29,845
editing one copy while later opening
an older copy somewhere else.

75
00:04:31,672 --> 00:04:35,921
Our partner should suggest clearer wording
without rewriting the handout directly.

76
00:04:36,072 --> 00:04:38,052
Viewer access usually allows reading.

77
00:04:38,852 --> 00:04:42,479
Commenter access, when supported,
allows suggestions through comments.

78
00:04:43,512 --> 00:04:45,844
Editor access permits
changes to the content.

79
00:04:46,872 --> 00:04:49,732
Choose the smallest role
that fits the task, inspect

80
00:04:49,732 --> 00:04:53,172
the recipient carefully,
and have the partner verify that the file

81
00:04:53,172 --> 00:04:57,032
opens with the expected controls.
A link that works for you

82
00:04:57,032 --> 00:05:01,552
may still fail for someone else. Also
distinguish read-only from encryption.

83
00:05:01,552 --> 00:05:06,772
Read-only limits editing; it does not
necessarily prevent someone from reading

84
00:05:06,772 --> 00:05:11,692
or copying the information. Pause and
choose a role for someone who only

85
00:05:11,692 --> 00:05:16,172
needs to read the finished handout.
Viewer is the appropriate starting point.

86
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:19,200
Sync is convenient because

87
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:22,260
a change can travel between a
device and an online service.

88
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:25,256
The same convenience can carry a deletion.

89
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:29,620
Watch the practice file disappear
from one location and then the other.

90
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:33,440
That is why sync alone
is not a separate backup.

91
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:38,260
A backup provides another recovery
copy under an appropriate plan.

92
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:41,400
Version history may
preserve earlier content,

93
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:45,580
while a recycle bin may retain a
deleted file for a limited time.

94
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:49,459
Availability and retention
depend on the service.

95
00:05:49,720 --> 00:05:52,620
Do not test deletion with
your only important file.

96
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:54,640
Use the fictional

97
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:55,640
copy and explain

98
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:59,785
which protection would help if the mistake
spread through every synced location.

99
00:06:01,614 --> 00:06:03,214
First identify the problem.

100
00:06:04,374 --> 00:06:06,654
If the whole file is
missing after deletion,

101
00:06:07,094 --> 00:06:09,426
inspect the appropriate
trash or recycle bin.

102
00:06:10,194 --> 00:06:14,074
If the file exists but its wording
changed, inspect version history.

103
00:06:15,194 --> 00:06:19,443
Preview the earlier version and coordinate
with collaborators before restoring it.

104
00:06:20,254 --> 00:06:23,259
You do not want to replace
useful work someone just added.

105
00:06:24,314 --> 00:06:25,774
Compression is a separate tool:

106
00:06:26,554 --> 00:06:30,494
a ZIP file can package several files
for transfer and may reduce their size.

107
00:06:31,294 --> 00:06:34,314
It is not automatically
encryption or a backup plan.

108
00:06:35,374 --> 00:06:40,234
After extracting a practice ZIP, open a
file to confirm the contents are usable.

109
00:06:41,294 --> 00:06:45,335
Choose the tool that matches the problem
rather than trusting a familiar icon.

110
00:06:46,910 --> 00:06:51,130
Pause and build a small resource
folder for your chosen community task.

111
00:06:51,730 --> 00:06:56,210
Include a source note, a fictional
handout, and a clear file name.

112
00:06:56,990 --> 00:06:59,011
Tell a partner which fact you verified,

113
00:06:59,710 --> 00:07:03,970
why that source is appropriate,
and which permission fits the review task.

114
00:07:04,970 --> 00:07:06,070
Then explain how

115
00:07:06,070 --> 00:07:08,410
you would recover a deleted file and how

116
00:07:08,410 --> 00:07:10,690
that differs from
restoring earlier wording.

117
00:07:11,350 --> 00:07:13,970
If an account or sharing
feature is unavailable,

118
00:07:14,450 --> 00:07:18,410
demonstrate the decision with the
course simulator and label it simulated.

119
00:07:19,890 --> 00:07:22,850
For your next independent task,
reuse this sequence:

120
00:07:23,850 --> 00:07:24,930
ask a focused question,

121
00:07:25,910 --> 00:07:32,730
check the evidence, record a trail,
organize the file, and verify access.
